    "evidence_text": "The corpus is compiled from web sources and consists of approximately 211,000 sentences.\n\nThe focus of this publication is on representing such information as an extension to an existing corpus standard.\n\nReadability measures, in their traditional form, take into account only surface\n\nTo the authors' knowledge, no productive system exists for German.\n\nThe importance of considering such information has repeatedly been asserted theoretically , , .\n\nThe remainder of this paper is structured as\n\nThe corpus additionally contains monolingual-only data (simplified German).\n\nThe corpus newly contains information on\n\ntext structure, typography, and images.\n\nFor the webpages, a static dump of all documents was created.\n\nFollowing this, the documents were manually checked to verify the language.\n\nThe main content was subsequently\n\nextracted, i.e., HTML markup and boilerplate removed using the Beautiful Soup library for Python.\n\nSimilarly, image information (content, position, and dimensions of an image) was preserved.\n\nThe toolkit produces output in an XML format (TETML).\n\nFigure FIGREF11 shows an example of OLAC metadata.\n\nlemmas, sentences, and dependency parses.\n\nTCF does not readily accommodate the incorporation of all of these types of information.\n\nFigure FIGREF34 shows a sample corpus annotation.\n\nTogether,\n\nTable TABREF36 shows the corpus profile.\n\nThe average sentence length is almost equal (approx. 11 tokens).\n\nBuilding a Corpus for Automatic Readability Assessment and Automatic Text Simplification of German\n\nTo our knowledge, this is the first study to deliver empirical proof of the relevance of such features.\n\nThe corpus presented in this paper additionally contains information on text structure, typography, and images.\n\nThese features have been shown to be indicative of simple vs. complex texts both theoretically and, using the corpus described in this paper,\n\nempirically.\n\nA set of parallel documents used in machine translation additionally requires sentence alignments, which are still missing from our corpus.",
